$ satlink net -h
exit status 0
--- stdout
Use a data call as an IP link that only this program can use

Usage: satlink net [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  status   Bring the link up, show what was negotiated, and hang up
  resolve  Look up a host name over the link
  get      Fetch an http:// or https:// URL and print its body
  tcp      Connect to HOST PORT and relay standard input and output, like netcat
  help     Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --number <NUMBER>            Number to dial for the data call [env: SATLINK_DATA_NUMBER=12345] [default: 008816000025]
  -p, --port <PORT>                Serial device (auto-detected by USB vendor ID when omitted) [env: SATLINK_PORT=]
      --allow <RULE>               Also allow a destination such as `example.com:443` (repeatable); the command's own target is always allowed
  -b, --baud <BAUD>                Serial port speed, in bits per second [default: 19200]
      --simulate[=<SIMULATE>]      Use a simulated handset instead of a real one, with Iridium's real timing (the default) or instant responses [possible values: iridium, instant]
      --username <USERNAME>        PPP user name (Iridium's gateway accepts any) [default: ""]
      --password <PASSWORD>        PPP password (Iridium's gateway accepts any) [env: SATLINK_PASSWORD] [default: ""]
  -v, --verbose...                 Log what the link is doing (-v), in more detail (-vv, -vvv)
      --idle-secs <IDLE_SECS>      Hang up after this many seconds without traffic [default: 300]
      --max-minutes <MAX_MINUTES>  Longest the call may last, in minutes, hanging up included [default: 60]
      --max-kb <MAX_KB>            Hang up after this many kilobytes of traffic
  -h, --help                       Print help (see more with '--help')
--- stderr
